references: <utfg26n3k8k.fsf@icw.EECS.Berkeley.EDU> <4ss7rt$sm1@vixen.cso.uiuc.edu> <utfhgr1t1aa.fsf@susie.EECS.Berkeley.EDU>
organization: University Communications, Inc.
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.unix.osf.misc


In article <utfhgr1t1aa.fsf@susie.EECS.Berkeley.EDU>,
Rajeev Kumar Ranjan <rajeev@susie.EECS.Berkeley.EDU> wrote:
>   From: peltz@jaka.ece.uiuc.edu (Steve Peltz)
>   Keywords: Cc:
>   >The compile command I used is: 	cc -g test.c -lpthreads
>
>   Instead of -lpthreads, just use -threads (or -pthread if you upgrade to
>   DU 4.0).
>
>The OS version that I have (OSF1 V3.2 41.64 alpha)  does not have
>libthread.a. But is it certain that the presence of libthread.a will
>solve the problem?

Use -threads, NOT -lthreads or -lthread or -lpthreads or anything else.
-threads is a flag to cc that tells it to use the thread-safe versions
of libraries. When you explicitly specified libpthreads.a, you got some
thread-safe versions and some non-thread versions, and the two sets of
incompatible routines obviously don't work very well together.

-pthread is similar (but only available on DU 4.0), but specifies to use
the final version of the POSIX threads interface. -threads uses a draft
version (-threads is still available in 4.0).



