>We're running DEC UNIX 3.2C, on a DEC Alpha 7000.  We tried shutting down 
>the system for maintenance using the command: "shutdown -h now", but 
>the system would not shutdown, like it normally does.  The shutdown
>command remains on the process list, along with all of the other processes
>it should've killed.  It shows a status of "U >+" for that process.  I
>could force a shutdown, but I'm curious to understand what is going on here.
>I'd appreciate any info, or possible causes, etc. 

One theory is that it's shutdown's notification of users that hangs the
process.  It may find an erroneous utmp entry showing a user logged
into a pseudo-tty: it hangs because there isn't a process on the master
side of the pty.

It may help to check the utmp file from time to time and clear these
incorrect entries.  I have a program to do this.
	ftp.ucs.mun.ca:pub/unix/digital/fixutmp.tar.Z

A loosely related problem: I've also noticed odd problems when X
windows creates the ":0" entry in utmp.  C2 security wouldn't let root
login on console if "/dev/:0" existed.  I believe /bin/wall and/or
/bin/dump would create the file.

The confirmation (and solution) of these problems will be much
appreciated.

Paul Fardy
